  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 155 AM MDT Mon Aug 17 2026\n\nFlow aloft has turned more zonal, bringing drier air into the area.\nThis, combined with lower dewpoints at the surface, will \ndecrease our precipitable water values to near normal for this \nafternoon. High res models are showing a dryline forming this \nafternoon somewhere near or to the east of the I-25 corridor. \nDewpoints will be in the 30s on the west side and 50s to low 60s \non the east side. Coverage of showers and storms looks much lower\nfor today, but if an isolated shower or storm can get going this \nafternoon, the location of the dryline will play a role in the \npotential hazards associated with that activity. To the west of \nthe dryline, we'll be more worried about virga leading to gusty \nwinds. To the east of the dryline, better instability and shear \ncould allow for an isolated strong to severe storm to develop. The\nmain threats will be strong winds and hail, but we could also see\nsome heavy rainfall and localized flash flooding where the \ndewpoints and moisture is the highest. \n\nA weak cold front is forecast to move through the plains Tuesday \nmorning and afternoon. The cold front will help to limit warming \nat the surface for a good portion of our area, even with the \nexpanding thermal ridge. The temperatures will warm the most in \nour southern counties (especially southern Lincoln) due to the \ndelayed impact of the front. Moisture will be higher behind the \nfront and winds will be breezy from the east/northeast. This could\nlead to some decent upslope in the mid to late afternoon. Shear \nwill be strong (~40kts at 0-6km) behind the front, but our potential\nlimiting factor when it comes to severe weather will be the lower\ninstability values. MLCAPEs are forecast to generally be below \n500 J/kg, with the exception of our far eastern plains counties, \nwhere there some potential for an isolated severe storm. A few \nmodels are keeping isolated convective activity going into the \nlate evening and potentially even the overnight hours, as residual\nboundaries lead to surface convergence. So, this is something \nwe'll have to keep an eye on.\n\nOn Wednesday, temperatures will be slightly cooler and there's \npotential for scattered showers and thunderstorms. Low level \nmoisture will remain elevated behind the front and easterly surface \nwinds will provide another day of upslope flow. Aloft, an upper \nlevel disturbance will move through providing additional lift. \nShear will remain high and instability looks to increase slightly,\nso we could see a few strong to severe thunderstorms in the \nplains. \n\nUpper level ridging will build over the area Thursday into the \nweekend. Temperatures will go back above normal, into the mid 80s to \nmid 90s across the plains. Rain chances will decrease for Thursday \nand Friday, with a low chance (<30%) for some isolated activity \nin the afternoons. PWs do start to increase for the weekend, with \na little disturbance or two aloft, increasing our rain chances.",
